  {
   "version": "v0.1.0",
   "title": "Project kickoff: design, references, ground rules",
   "date": "2026-11-22",
   "html": "<p><strong>What</strong></p><ul><li><code>sketch.md</code>: the full design scratchpad: core thesis, v0 architecture, sub-quadratic parts bin, speculative decoding, the Rival Loop, data &amp; evals, local hardware feasibility, decisions log.</li><li><code>refs/</code>: 55 papers across 9 topics, with an index (<code>refs/README.md</code>) and a re-runnable fetch script. PDFs are git-ignored.</li><li><code>README.md</code> (pitch), <code>AGENTS.md</code> (repo rules), this devlog.</li></ul><p><strong>Decisions</strong></p><ul><li>Target is a <strong>regular causal LLM</strong>: loops add per-token latent depth.</li><li>Device: the laptop. No hard memory target: we explore the design space with a configurable sweep harness.</li><li>v0 layout: prelude (SWA) → core × r (GDN, GDN, SWA) → coda (one full-attention layer, run once). No quadratic layer inside the loop.</li><li>Intermediate loops decode through a cheap exit head, never the coda.</li></ul><p><strong>Findings</strong></p><ul><li>Native Windows ARM64 has CPU-only PyTorch. <strong>WSL Ubuntu sees the GPU</strong> and Linux aarch64 CUDA wheels exist, so we train in WSL.</li><li>Closest prior art: <em>Tiny Recursive Reasoning with Mamba-2 Attention Hybrid</em> (arXiv 2602.12078). Must read before building.</li></ul><p><strong>Next</strong></p><ul><li>Read the closest prior art, then lock the novelty story.</li><li>Set up the WSL environment (PyTorch CUDA, check <code>flash-linear-attention</code> on aarch64 + Blackwell).</li><li>Smoke-test harness: TinyStories, ~10M params, full precision, loop vs no-loop.</li></ul>"
  }
